  "worked_solution": "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}={z}^{4},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={z}^{4}, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =({z}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={z}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
